### What can be applied and what needs further source material

The supplied material supports distinguishing marginal correction from revenue raising, explaining the weighted Ramsey/Pigou expression, evaluating its $MCPF=1$ algebraic case and separating weak/strong double dividend from tax interaction. Numerical application of the expanded Sandmo formula and a proof of the p. 54 conditions require defined tax normalization, missing utility notation and full model assumptions. Those strands remain source gaps, while the mechanism and conditional comparisons above are usable. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Economics_of_Green_Deal/Week_2/01_Regulation_Externality-1.pdf#page=49|Lecture, physical PDF pp. 49–54]]


## Worked example

O011-W1 / item_version 1, worked_example. **Prompt:** Symbolic lecture decomposition $\theta_D=(1-\eta)R+\eta P$, η=1/MCPF. Hypothetically declared MCPF=1 (no empirical calibration): derive the symbolic weights and the special MCPF 1 case, identify each component and revenue-name ambiguity, and explain the three tax goals, excess burden and lump-sum income/substitution distinction. Separate the commodity model from labour-interaction reasoning. For a labelled hypothetical reform, environmental welfare gain=12, recycling nonenvironmental gain=4, tax-interaction loss=6; a lump-sum return of the same revenue has zero recycling gain with the same interaction loss. Apply weak versus strong double-dividend comparisons and total versus nonenvironmental changes without counting revenue twice. A claim confuses environmental gain with an extra efficiency gain. State what missing symbols/base/assumptions prevent numerical expanded Sandmo application or a universal final-slide theorem.

**Steps and interpretation:** η=1/1, Ramsey weight1−1/1=0.000000, Pigou weight1/1=1.000000; θ_D remains that symbolic combination of R andP, not a calibrated tax rate/base. IfMCPF1, Ramsey weight0/Pigou1 soθ_D=P. R denotes Ramsey component here but elsewhere revenue requirement; MCPF welfare-loss/additional-revenue ratio uses revenueR. Goals correct marginal prices, change behaviour and raise revenue for public goods; revenue transfer is not extra welfare equal to collection. Excess burden is distortion beyond transfer; a behaviour-independent lump-sum tax has no substitution effect but may have income effect. Simplified inverse-elasticity rule is conditional, not universal demand/distribution prescription. Sandmo displayed commodity model excludes labour taxation; labour real-wage/supply tax-interaction mechanism is additional discussion. Recycling comparison: reform nonenvironmental 4−6=-2; lump-sum−6; recycling improves relative efficiency by4, a weak comparison of the same revenue. Strong extra nonenvironmental gain holds in this declared arithmetic only if-2>0: no. Total including environmental gain=12+4−6=10; a positive total does not itself establish strong dividend. The toy accounting is not an empirical result or general equilibrium proof. N,u_ND,u_D, utility model and tax normalization/base are undeclared in expansion; no guessed meaning/rate. Final separability/one-distortionary-tax qualifications lack full model/proof, so no universal weak/strong guarantee.

